      "title": "Business Services Coordinator",
      "description": "UNC Charlotte Applicant Site | Business Services Coordinator Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Frequently Asked Questions Application Process State of NC Salary Chart Campus Security Report Student Employment Business Services Coordinator Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. This posting will be used to fill two positions: 004517 & 001828. This position is being reopened to attract a larger candidate pool. If you have already applied for this position, you do not need to reapply as your application is still valid. The finalist will be subject to a Criminal Background Check and/or a Motor Vehicle Report The Search Committee will not contact references without first verifying permission with the finalist. Please ensure your application reflects the knowledge, skills, abilities, and experiences to support your answers to the supplemental questions. Do not write ‘see resume’ on your application when completing the job duties section. UNC Charlotte Benefits Information: https://hr.charlotte.edu/benefits/benefits-overview/ Position Information General Information Vacancy Open to All Candidates Employment Type Time Limited - Full-time If time-limited, note appointment end date 08/31/2029 Hours per week 40 Months per year 12 Position Number 004517 NC Salary Grade Equivalency NC11 Classification Title Business Services Coordinator - Advanced Working Title Business Services Coordinator Salary  Range $44,640 - $55,000 Anticipate Hiring Range $44,640 - $55,000 FLSA Status Non Exempt Division Academic Affairs Department College of Engineering (Col) Work Unit Student Dev & Success Work Schedule 8:00 am – 5:00 pm; Monday – Friday, with occasional evening and weekend hours required, as necessary. A flexible work schedule may be available with supervisor approval. Primary Purpose of Position This position provides full support for The William States Lee College of Engineering’s Office of Student Development and Success ( OSDS ). Manages budget, personnel, and any business processes, and assists faculty, students, staff, and visitors. Reports to the Director of OSDS . Handles routine office functions and ad hoc projects as assigned. Handles correspondence and confidential information. Identifies inefficiencies and opportunities. Minimum Education/Experience Required Minimum Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree in business administration or a related discipline; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. All degrees must be received from appropriately accredited institutions. Note: Some state job postings state you can qualify by an ‘equivalent combination of education and experience.’ If that language appears above, then you may qualify through EITHER years of education OR years of directly related experience, OR a combination of both. Essential Job Duties Manages budget tasks including: budget planning, reconciliation, and reporting Collaborates with other Office Administrators on purchasing and reconciliation processes Serve as cost object approver for faculty and staff travel to ensure proper fund use Manage student hires of undergraduate and graduate TAs as well as peer mentors. Monitor and reconcile student work salary for budget tracking Personnel processes including adjunct faculty hiring, faculty reappointments, summer salary, and administrative stipends Uses all fund types to support the department, including but not limited to state funds, fee funds, grants, discretionary and trust funds. Handles correspondence and confidential information. Manages records and fund activity. Processes paperwork and deposits. Interprets data. Identifies inefficiencies and opportunities. Promotes cross-training among staff. Assists Dean’s Office on Academic Affairs reporting and other items as needed. Uses a variety of university systems. Uses University Purchasing Card. Uses Microsoft Office Suite and Google Suite. Other Work Responsibilities Aids in the resolution of issues encountered by faculty, staff, students, and visitors. Understands university guidelines, policies, and procedures and conveys this information clearly. Ensures business processes comply. Other duties include event support. Departmental Preferred Experience, Skills, Training/Education Department Preferred Experience, Skills Accounts reconciliation, business processing, office management experience. Ability to learn University systems and procedures, including 49er Mart, Concur, Banner Finance, and payment request forms Experience generating reports and creating spreadsheets. Experience with hiring and tracking hourly student wages Excellent communication and problem resolution. Necessary Licenses or Certifications Work Location Smith Posting date 08/26/2026 Closing date 09/17/2026 Proposed Hire Date 10/01/2026 Contact Information Special Notes to Applicants This posting will be used to fill two positions: 004517 & 001828. This position is being reopened to attract a larger candidate pool. If you have already applied for this position, you do not need to reapply as your application is still valid. The finalist will be subject to a Criminal Background Check and/or a Motor Vehicle Report The Search Committee will not contact references without first verifying permission with the finalist. Please ensure your application reflects the knowledge, skills, abilities, and experiences to support your answers to the supplemental questions. Do not write ‘see resume’ on your application when completing the job duties section. UNC Charlotte Benefits Information: https://hr.charlotte.edu/benefits/benefits-overview/ Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this employment opportunity? UNC Charlotte Website HERC Job Board Inside Higher Education Mitratech Circa (formerly known as Local JobNetwork) Another Website Agency Referral Advertisement/Publication Personal Referral Other Where did you learn about this posting? (Open Ended Question) * Do you have a bachelor’s degree in business administration or a related discipline, or an equivalent combination of education and experience? All degrees must be received from appropriately accredited institutions. Yes, at least a bachelor's degree in business administration or a related discipline. Yes, an associate's degree business administration or a related discipline and two years of related experience. Yes, a high school diploma/GED and four years of related work experience. No * Do you have experience in accounts reconciliation, business processing, office management, or human resources? If so please explain your experience. (Open Ended Question) * Have you used systems such as 49er Mart, Concur, Banner Finance, or payment request forms? Please explain your experience. (Open Ended Question) * Do you have experience generating reports and creating spreadsheets in Excel and Google Sheets? Please explain your experience. (Open Ended Question) * Do you have professional experience with hiring and tracking hourly student wages? Please explain: (Open Ended Question) Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume / Curriculum Vitae Cover Letter / Letter of Interest Contact Information for References Optional Documents 9201 University City Blvd \r\nCharlotte, NC 28223 © 2026 The University of North Carolina at Charlotte. All rights reserved. To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/70495": {
      "title": "Position Information",
      "description": "UNC Charlotte Applicant Site | Business Analyst I Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Frequently Asked Questions Application Process State of NC Salary Chart Campus Security Report Student Employment Business Analyst I Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. The finalist will be subject to a Criminal Background Check and/or a Motor Vehicle Report The Search Committee will not contact references without first verifying permission with the finalist. Please ensure your resume reflects the knowledge, skills, abilities, and experiences to support your answers to the supplemental questions. UNC Charlotte Benefits Information: https://hr.charlotte.edu/benefits/benefits-overview/ Position Information General Information Position Number 009615 Vacancy Open to All Candidates Working Title Business Analyst I Position Designation EHRA Non-Faculty Employment Type Permanent - Full-time Months per Year 12 Work Schedule Typical University hours are Monday – Friday, 8:00 am – 5:00 pm. Hours may differ and will be determined by the supervisor. Hours per week 40 FLSA Status Exempt Division Business Affairs Department Facilities Management (Adm) Work Location Facilities Management and Public Safety Salary Range $63,992 Primary Purpose of Department The primary purpose of the Performance & amp; Analytics unit is to enhance operational performance within Facilities Management by integrating quality assurance, data analysis, and process improvement into a unified, closed-loop system. The unit systematically captures and analyzes performance data, transforming field observations and operational information into actionable insights that identify trends, risks, and high-impact opportunities. Functioning as a performance engine for the organization, the unit leads targeted, data-driven improvement initiatives that proactively address issues, optimize processes, and reduce operational inefficiencies. Through a continuous feedback approach, the unit ensures that improvement efforts are effectively implemented, measured, and sustained. This function serves as a central driver of consistency, efficiency, and informed decision-making, strengthening overall operational effectiveness and alignment with organizational goals. Primary Purpose of Position This position serves as an advanced analytical partner within Facilities Management, independently evaluating complex operational and financial information to identify trends, performance gaps, risks, and opportunities for improvement. The position determines appropriate analytical approaches, evaluation criteria, and methods for interpreting data based on the nature of the business question and available information. The position translates analytical findings into meaningful visualizations, insights, and recommendations that support leadership decision- making, resource prioritization, service delivery, and operational improvement. In addition to analytical responsibilities, the position leads assigned process improvement initiatives and works collaboratively with operational staff, process improvement practitioners, and other subject matter experts to evaluate processes, identify root causes and inefficiencies, develop improvement recommendations, and assess the effectiveness of implemented changes. The position exercises independent judgment in determining analytical methods, evaluating alternatives, resolving complex data issues, and recommending actions within established organizational objectives and policies. Summary of Position Responsibilities Determines the appropriate visualization methods based on the nature of the data, objective, and audience. Designs and refines performance dashboards Applies principles of data visualization and visual storytelling, and develops visual materials that support process improvement initiatives to include current-state assessments, performance comparisons, root cause analysis, and measurement of improvement results. Analyze operational and financial data to identify trends, risks, and opportunities for improvement across Facilities Management Develop and maintain performance metrics, KPIs, and dashboards to support decision-making Translate complex data into clear, actionable insights and recommendations for leadership Integrate analytics with quality review findings and process improvement initiatives Evaluate work management data (e.g., backlog, response times, labor utilization) to assess service delivery performance Support strategic and operational planning through data analysis and scenario evaluation Ensure data accuracy and consistency by identifying and resolving data integrity issues Prepare reports, dashboards, and decision-support materials for leadership and stakeholders Serve as a consultative partner to operational units in interpreting performance data Support performance monitoring activities, including data collection, validation, and reporting processes Minimum Education/Experience Bachelor’s degree in Business Administration, Public Administration, Data Analytics, Information Systems, Operations Management, or a related field; or an equivalent combination of education and experience. Experience in data analysis, business analysis, operational performance evaluation, data visualization, or a related analytical field. Experience interpreting complex data and developing insights or recommendations to support operational or organizational decision-making. Preferred Education, Knowledge, Skills and Experience Experience in facilities management, maintenance operations, higher education, or similar complex service environments. Experience developing and applying KPIs, performance metrics, benchmarking methods, and operational dashboards. Experience with data visualization and visual storytelling using Tableau, Power BI, Excel, or similar tools. Proficiency with analytical and data-management tools such as Python, HTML , Excel, Smartsheet, Tableau, Power BI, or similar platforms. Experience leading or participating in Lean Six Sigma, process improvement, business process analysis, or continuous improvement initiatives. Experience collaborating with process improvement practitioners or subject matter experts to evaluate and improve operational processes. Familiarity with work management, CMMS , EAM , or other enterprise operational systems. Experience identifying and resolving data integrity issues across multiple systems or data sources. Necessary Certifications/Licenses Preferred Certifications/Licenses Special Notes to Applicants The finalist will be subject to a Criminal Background Check and/or a Motor Vehicle Report The Search Committee will not contact references without first verifying permission with the finalist. Please ensure your resume reflects the knowledge, skills, abilities, and experiences to support your answers to the supplemental questions. UNC Charlotte Benefits Information: https://hr.charlotte.edu/benefits/benefits-overview/ Posting Open Date 09/10/2026 Posting Close Date 09/17/2026 Open Until Filled No Proposed Hire Date 10/16/2026 If time-limited please indicate appointment end date Contact Information Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this employment opportunity? UNC Charlotte Website HERC Job Board Inside Higher Education Mitratech Circa (formerly known as Local JobNetwork) Another Website Agency Referral Advertisement/Publication Personal Referral Other Where did you learn about this posting? (Open Ended Question) * Do you have a bachelor’s degree in Business Administration, Public Administration, Data Analytics, Information Systems, Operations Management, or a related field, or an equivalent combination of education and experience? Yes, at least a bachelor’s degree in Business Administration, Public Administration, Data Analytics, Information Systems, Operations Management, or a related field. Yes, an associate's degree in Business Administration, Public Administration, Data Analytics, Information Systems, Operations Management, or a related field and two years of relevant experience. Yes, a high school diploma/GED and four years of relevant experience. No * Do you have experience in data analysis, business analysis, or operational performance evaluation? Yes No * Do you have experience interpreting complex data and developing insights or recommendations to support operational or organizational decision-making? (Open Ended Question) Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume / Curriculum Vitae Cover Letter / Letter of Interest Contact Information for References Optional Documents 9201 University City Blvd \r\nCharlotte, NC 28223 © 2026 The University of North Carolina at Charlotte. All rights reserved. To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/70486": {
      "title": "Postdoc Research Fellow; School of Architecture",
      "description": "UNC Charlotte Applicant Site | Postdoc Research Fellow; School of Architecture Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Frequently Asked Questions Application Process State of NC Salary Chart Campus Security Report Student Employment Postdoc Research Fellow; School of Architecture Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Please upload the following documents with your electronic submission: 1. Cover letter 2. A detailed Curriculum Vitae 3. Two reference letters 4. A research statement (not more than one page) describing your previous research work and future goals. 5. Work sample portfolio and/or selected publications The candidate chosen for this position will be required to provide an official transcript of their highest earned degree. The candidate chosen for this position will be subject to a criminal background check. The University of North Carolina at Charlotte is an equal opportunity employer and does not discriminate against employees or applicants for employment based on race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, age, national origin, physical or mental disability, veteran status, or any other protected status. In keeping with this commitment, UNC Charlotte ensures that persons of any background are invited, included, and treated equally in the terms and conditions of employment. Position Information General Information Position Number POST40 Working Title Postdoc Research Fellow; School of Architecture Division Academic Affairs Department Col of Arts & Architecture (Col) Work Unit School of Architecture Work Location Integrated Design Research Lab (IDRL), Storrs Building Vacancy Open To All Candidates Position Designation Post Doc Employment Type Temporary - Full-time Hours per week 40 Work Schedule Varies Pay Rate $54,000/yr Minimum Experience/Education The Postdoctoral appointee must have recently (within the last eight years) been awarded a Ph.D. or equivalent doctorate (e.g., Sc.D., M.D.) Departmental Preferred Experience, Skills, Training/Education: Ph.D. in Architectural Technology, Biology/Biotechnology, Mechanical or Electrical Engineering, Chemical Engineering, Materials Science, Environmental Engineering, or a related field relevant to bio-integrated and regenerative building technologies. Preferred Knowledge and Competencies Candidates should demonstrate knowledge or research experience in at least two of the following areas: • Bio-integrated building systems, photobioreactors, microalgae cultivation, or biological process systems. • Building environmental systems, heat transfer, fluid flow, thermal systems, or building-energy performance. • Low-carbon materials, concrete, biomaterials, or material characterization. • Sensors, environmental monitoring, data acquisition, controls, or AI-enabled building systems. • Experimental design, laboratory testing, prototype development, and quantitative data analysis. • Research documentation, peer-reviewed publication, and grant/proposal development. • Experience with hands-on prototyping, experimental research, interdisciplinary collaboration, and data analysis using tools such as Python, MATLAB , R, or equivalent software is preferred. Applicants are not expected to have expertise in every research area. The successful candidate’s responsibilities will be aligned with their primary expertise while contributing to interdisciplinary research across architecture, engineering, biology, and materials. A Postdoctoral Fellow (\"\"postdoc\"\") is a professional apprenticeship designed to provide recent Ph.D. recipients with an opportunity to develop further the research skills acquired in their doctoral programs or to learn new research techniques, in preparation for an academic or research career. In the process of further developing their own research skills, it is expected that Postdoctoral Fellows will also play a significant role in the performance of research at the University and augment the role of graduate faculty in providing research instruction to graduate students. A Postdoctoral Fellow works under the supervision of a regular faculty member, who serves as a mentor to the Fellow, and it is expected that the faculty mentor will impart the realities, and variety, of scientific careers, and will encourage experiences outside the laboratory to broaden postdocs’ aspirations. Within the confines of the particular research focus assigned by that faculty member, the Postdoctoral Fellow functions with a considerable degree of independence and has the freedom (and is expected) to publish the results of his or her research or scholarship during the period of appointment. Thus, the role of Postdoctoral Fellows is clearly differentiated from full-time technical employees. Postdoc appointments are characterized by all of the following conditions: • the appointee was recently (within the last eight years) awarded a Ph.D. or equivalent doctorate (e.g., Sc.D., M.D.); • the appointment is temporary, for up to one year at a time, with renewal possible; • the total appointment is limited to five years, including time spent in previous postdoctoral appointments at other institutions; • the appointment involves substantially full-time research or scholarship at 1.0 FTE ; • the appointment helps the Postdoctoral Fellow to prepare for a fulltime academic and/or research career; • the appointee works under the supervision of a faculty member; and • the appointee has the freedom and is expected, to publish the results of their research or scholarship during the period of appointment. Duties and Responsibilities The Postdoctoral Fellow will conduct interdisciplinary research in regenerative building technologies within the Integrated Design Research Lab. Responsibilities will include: • Prototype development and testing: Design, fabricate, operate, and evaluate bio-integrated building systems, microalgae façade systems, and/or low-carbon material prototypes; develop experimental protocols and document results. • Data analysis and system evaluation: Collect and analyze thermal, biological, environmental, and material-performance data; evaluate system performance and identify opportunities for optimization. • Research development and tech translation: Lead or support research in microalgae cultivation, thermal-fluid integration, sensing/controls, and/or low-carbon materials based on the Fellow’s expertise, and translate laboratory findings toward pilot-scale applications. • Publications and grants: Prepare peer-reviewed manuscripts, technical reports, presentations, and contribute to external grant proposals. • Collaboration and mentoring: Coordinate with academic and industry partners and mentor undergraduate and graduate researchers. Individual Development Plan ( IDP ): The faculty mentor and Postdoctoral Fellow will develop an IDP at the beginning of the appointment. The IDP will establish specific annual goals for research, publications, grant development, mentoring, professional networking, and preparation for the Fellow’s future academic, research, or industry career. The IDP milestones will be aligned with the Fellow’s assigned responsibilities, including prototype development and testing, data analysis, peer-reviewed publications, grant development, and interdisciplinary collaboration. Other Work/Responsibilities The postdoctoral fellow may also contribute to related IDRL research and prototyping, including solar technologies and other advanced building systems, as project needs and expertise align. Necessary Licenses or Certifications Proposed Hire Date 10/01/2026 Contact Information Dr. Kyounghee Kim Kkim33@charlotte.edu 7046870112 Expected Length of Assignment 12 months Posting Open Date 09/08/2026 Posting Close Date Special Notes to Applicants Please upload the following documents with your electronic submission: 1. Cover letter 2. A detailed Curriculum Vitae 3. Two reference letters 4. A research statement (not more than one page) describing your previous research work and future goals. 5. Work sample portfolio and/or selected publications The candidate chosen for this position will be required to provide an official transcript of their highest earned degree. The candidate chosen for this position will be subject to a criminal background check. The University of North Carolina at Charlotte is an equal opportunity employer and does not discriminate against employees or applicants for employment based on race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, age, national origin, physical or mental disability, veteran status, or any other protected status. In keeping with this commitment, UNC Charlotte ensures that persons of any background are invited, included, and treated equally in the terms and conditions of employment. Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). How did you hear about this employment opportunity? UNC Charlotte Website HERC Job Board Inside Higher Education Mitratech Circa (formerly known as Local JobNetwork) Another Website Agency Referral Advertisement/Publication Personal Referral Other Where did you learn about this posting? (Open Ended Question) Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter / Letter of Interest Resume / Curriculum Vitae Contact Information for References Research Interest Statement Online Portfolio Optional Documents Other Document 9201 University City Blvd \r\nCharlotte, NC 28223 © 2026 The University of North Carolina at Charlotte. All rights reserved. To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/70506": {
      "title": "Accommodation Coordinator",
      "description": "UNC Charlotte Applicant Site | Accommodation Coordinator Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Frequently Asked Questions Application Process State of NC Salary Chart Campus Security Report Student Employment Accommodation Coordinator Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. The finalist will be subject to a Criminal Background Check and/or a Motor Vehicle Report The Search Committee will not contact references without first verifying permission with the finalist . Please ensure your resume reflects the knowledge, skills, abilities, and experiences to support your answers to the supplemental questions. UNC Charlotte Benefits Information: https://hr.charlotte.edu/benefits/benefits-overview/ Position Information General Information Position Number 007974 Vacancy Open to All Candidates Working Title Accommodation Coordinator Position Designation EHRA Non-Faculty Employment Type Permanent - Full-time Months per Year 12 Work Schedule Normal Work Hours: Monday-Friday, 8:00 am – 5:00 pm; some nights and weekends required (subject to change based on program needs and University decisions). Hours per week 40 FLSA Status Exempt Division Academic Affairs Department University College (Adm) Work Location Fretwell 230 Salary Range $60,000 Primary Purpose of Department The Office of Accessibility and Accommodation, a Division of Academic Affairs, works to ensure that students with disabilities have access to education and campus life at UNC Charlotte. Through collaboration with the institution’s varied community, Accessibility and Accommodation facilitates accommodations, discourse, and engagement to promote a universally accessible learning environment for all. As part of a collaborative team working with a growing population of 1000 students, our approach is strength-based, promoting independence and self-advocacy. Primary Purpose of Position The Accommodation Coordinator in the Office of Accessibility and Accommodation is responsible for reviewing documentation from multiple sources, determining disability, and planning reasonable accommodations for students with disabilities at UNC Charlotte. This position is part administrative, part advocacy, and part outreach, and requires a strong knowledge of the ADA , Section 504, student rights, confidentiality practices, evaluation practices for disability documentation, and appropriate accommodations. Using a student-centered, inclusive approach, the Accommodation Coordinator manages a large student caseload and provides ongoing case management to new and returning students; maintains accurate case notes and keeps up-to-date electronic records; and works collaboratively with students, faculty/staff, and campus partners to help ensure that students have access to education and campus life at UNC Charlotte. Summary of Position Responsibilities Baseline Responsibilities Direct Student Access & Case Management: Conduct interactive intake meetings to determine disability status and plan/coordinate reasonable accommodations; deliver 1:1 ongoing support, connection, and advocacy for assigned caseload and walk-ins as necessary. Documentation & Records: Review documentation from multiple sources under ADA , ADAAA , and Section 504 guidelines; maintain up-to-date electronic records and accurate case notes in the departmental database system. Faculty & Campus Consultation: Partner with faculty and staff to facilitate accommodations, address implementation barriers, and ensure educational access. Department-Specific Responsibilities Campus Liaison, Committee Work, & Advocacy: Engage with campus partners to provide disability perspectives, elevate access initiatives, and promote universal design. Program Development & Continuous Improvement: Lead or assist in creating OAA initiatives, evaluating technology/practices, and recommending process enhancements. Professional Development & Research: Stay current on legal precedents, ADA compliance, assistive technologies, and emerging best practices in higher education disability services. Departmental & Campus Engagement Support core office operations (including final exam proctoring and administration), represent OAA at Orientation, Open House, and Commencement events, and perform other duties as assigned. Minimum Education/Experience Must have completed a Master’s Degree in a human services field, including social work, counseling, rehabilitation counseling, or a related field. A minimum of one year of experience working with individuals with disabilities is required. Preferred Education, Knowledge, Skills and Experience Experience working with individuals with disabilities who are post-high school is preferable; experience working with individuals with disabilities in a higher education setting is highly desirable. Case management experience Excellent oral and written communication skills. Excellent collaborative, problem-solving, planning, and organizational skills are necessary. Working knowledge of the ADA and Section 504 as pertains to considering accommodations within a higher education setting. Knowledge of FERPA is helpful. Knowledge of assistive technology as accommodations is helpful. Necessary Certifications/Licenses Preferred Certifications/Licenses Special Notes to Applicants The finalist will be subject to a Criminal Background Check and/or a Motor Vehicle Report The Search Committee will not contact references without first verifying permission with the finalist . Please ensure your resume reflects the knowledge, skills, abilities, and experiences to support your answers to the supplemental questions. UNC Charlotte Benefits Information: https://hr.charlotte.edu/benefits/benefits-overview/ Posting Open Date 09/15/2026 Posting Close Date 10/05/2026 Open Until Filled No Proposed Hire Date If time-limited please indicate appointment end date Contact Information Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this employment opportunity? UNC Charlotte Website HERC Job Board Inside Higher Education Circa (formerly known as Local JobNetwork) Another Website Agency Referral Advertisement/Publication Personal Referral Other Where did you learn about this posting? (Open Ended Question) * Do you have a Master's Degree in a human services field, including social work, counseling, rehabilitation counseling, or a related field? Yes, I have a Master's Degree in a human services field, including social work, counseling, rehabilitation counseling, or a related field. No, I do not have a Master's Degree * Do you have a minimum of one year of experience working with individuals with disabilities? Yes, I have a minimum of one year of experience with individuals with disabilities. No, I do not Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume / Curriculum Vitae Cover Letter / Letter of Interest Contact Information for References Optional Documents Other Document 9201 University City Blvd \r\nCharlotte, NC 28223 © 2026 The University of North Carolina at Charlotte. All rights reserved. To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/70508": {
      "title": "Accommodation Coordinator",
      "description": "UNC Charlotte Applicant Site | Accommodation Coordinator Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Frequently Asked Questions Application Process State of NC Salary Chart Campus Security Report Student Employment Accommodation Coordinator Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. The finalist will be subject to a Criminal Background Check and/or a Motor Vehicle Report The Search Committee will not contact references without first verifying permission with the finalist . Please ensure your resume reflects the knowledge, skills, abilities, and experiences to support your answers to the supplemental questions. UNC Charlotte Benefits Information: https://hr.charlotte.edu/benefits/benefits-overview/ Position Information General Information Position Number 007973 Vacancy Open to All Candidates Working Title Accommodation Coordinator Position Designation EHRA Non-Faculty Employment Type Permanent - Full-time Months per Year 12 Work Schedule 8:00 am – 5:00 pm; Monday – Friday with occasional evening and weekend hours required, as necessary. The University College promotes a flexible and hybrid work environment wherever possible, after initial onboarding and training is completed. Hours per week 40 FLSA Status Exempt Division Academic Affairs Department University College (Adm) Work Location Fretwell 230 Salary Range $60,000 Primary Purpose of Department The Office of Accessibility and Accommodation, a Division of Academic Affairs, works to ensure that students with disabilities have access to education and campus life at UNC Charlotte. Through collaboration with the institution’s varied community, Accessibility and Accommodation facilitates accommodations, discourse, and engagement to promote a universally accessible learning environment for all. As part of a collaborative team working with growing population of 1000 students, our approach is strength-based, promoting independence and self-advocacy. Primary Purpose of Position The Accommodation Coordinator in the Office of Accessibility and Accommodation is responsible for determining eligibility for services; and, through the interactive process, planning reasonable accommodations for students with disabilities. at UNC Charlotte. This position is part administrative, advocate, and outreach, and requires a strong knowledge of the ADA , student rights, confidentiality practices, evaluation practices for disability documentation, and appropriate accommodations. Using a student-centered, inclusive approach, the Accommodation Coordinator:Works collaboratively with students, faculty/staff and campus partners to help ensure physical and programmatic access; maintains a student caseload and provides ongoing case management to new and returning students; and maintains accurate and up-to-date electronic records. Summary of Position Responsibilities Baseline Responsibilities Direct Student Access & Case Management: Conduct interactive intake meetings to determine disability status and plan/coordinate reasonable accommodations; deliver 1:1 ongoing support and connection and advocacy for assigned caseload and walk-ins as necessary. Documentation & Records: Review documentation from multiple sources under ADA , ADAAA , and Section 504 guidelines; maintain up-to-date electronic records and accurate case notes in departmental database system. Faculty & Campus Consultation: Partner with faculty and staff to facilitate accommodations, address implementation barriers, and ensure educational access. Department-Specific Responsibilities Campus Liaison, Committee Work, & Advocacy: Engage with campus partners to provide disability perspectives, elevate access initiatives, and promote universal design. Program Development & Continuous Improvement: Lead or assist in creating OAA initiatives, evaluating technology/practices, and recommending process enhancements. Professional Development & Research: Stay current on legal precedents, ADA compliance, assistive technologies, and emerging best practices in higher education disability services. Departmental & Campus Engagement Support core office operations (including final exam proctoring and administration), represent OAA at Orientation, Open House, and Commencement events, and perform other duties as assigned. Minimum Education/Experience Must have completed a Master’s Degree in a human services field, including social work, counseling, rehabilitation counseling, or a related field. A minimum of one year of experience working with individuals with disabilities is required. Preferred Education, Knowledge, Skills and Experience 2-years experience working with individuals with disabilities is preferred. Experience working with individuals with disabilities who are post-high school is preferable; experience working with individuals with disabilities in a higher education setting is highly desirable. Excellent oral and written communication skills. Excellent collaborative, problem-solving, planning and organizational skills necessary. Working knowledge of the ADA and Section 504 as pertains to considering accommodations within a higher education settings. Knowledge of FERPA helpful. Knowledge of assistive technology as accommodations helpful. Necessary Certifications/Licenses Preferred Certifications/Licenses Special Notes to Applicants The finalist will be subject to a Criminal Background Check and/or a Motor Vehicle Report The Search Committee will not contact references without first verifying permission with the finalist . Please ensure your resume reflects the knowledge, skills, abilities, and experiences to support your answers to the supplemental questions. UNC Charlotte Benefits Information: https://hr.charlotte.edu/benefits/benefits-overview/ Posting Open Date 09/15/2026 Posting Close Date 10/05/2026 Open Until Filled No Proposed Hire Date If time-limited please indicate appointment end date Contact Information Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this employment opportunity? UNC Charlotte Website HERC Job Board Inside Higher Education Circa (formerly known as Local JobNetwork) Another Website Agency Referral Advertisement/Publication Personal Referral Other Where did you learn about this posting? (Open Ended Question) * Do you have a Master's Degree in a human services field, including social work, counseling, rehabilitation counseling, or a related field? Yes, I have a Master's Degree in a human services field, including social work, counseling, rehabilitation counseling, or a related field. No, I do not have a Master's Degree * Do you have a minimum of one year of experience working with individuals with disabilities? Yes, I have a minimum of one year of experience with individuals with disabilities. No, I do not Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume / Curriculum Vitae Cover Letter / Letter of Interest Contact Information for References Optional Documents Other Document 9201 University City Blvd \r\nCharlotte, NC 28223 © 2026 The University of North Carolina at Charlotte. All rights reserved. To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
