    "https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/70641": {
      "title": "Associate Dean for Production Arts + Operations",
      "description": "UNC Charlotte Applicant Site | Associate Dean for Production Arts + Operations Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Frequently Asked Questions Application Process State of NC Salary Chart Campus Security Report Student Employment Associate Dean for Production Arts + Operations Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Position Information General Information Position Number 003744 Working Title Associate Dean for Production Arts + Operations Classification Title Associate Academic Dean College Col of Arts & Architecture (Col) Department College of Arts + Architecture Position Designation Faculty Employment Type Permanent - Full-time Months per year 12 Primary Purpose of Department The College of Arts + Architecture was formed in 2008 with the aim of combining the arts disciplines and architecture under one roof, bringing a critical mass of artists and designers together to elevate the College’s voice. As the only college of its kind in the UNC System, the COAA demonstrates UNC Charlotte’s commitment to arts and culture on campus and in the broader community, presenting exhibitions, lectures, and more than 100 performances each year. The College is home to three graduate degree programs, nine undergraduate programs, and certificate programs in Musical Theatre, Jazz, and Advanced Dance Studies, and a professional training certificate in dance in partnership with the Charlotte Ballet. The College is deeply committed to student retention and graduation and provides extensive resources, including academic and career advising, internships, and scholarships. Students and faculty all benefit from the College’s commitment to personalized advising from specialized, full-time professional academic advisors who work with students one-on-one throughout their course of study. Vacancy Open to All Candidates Description of Work Assist the Dean in the oversight of various duties, which may include but aren’t limited to, advancing research missions, supporting the undergraduate and graduate degree programs, faculty evaluation, faculty development, facilitate external support for undergraduate and graduate programs, curriculum development, course schedule planning, faculty/staff hiring and supervision, budget development, fiscal monitoring, and other related administrative activities with and for the assigned program areas. Reports directly to the Dean and may assume some duties of the Dean in their absence. Essential Duties and Responsibilities Essential Duties The Associate Dean for Production Arts + Operations works with the Chairs of the Dance, Music and Theatre Departments to schedule all internal and external performances (as well as curricular and programmatic events). The Associate Dean also supports instructional delivery in order to ensure the effective use of these spaces in a manner that advances departmental and College strategic goals and academic missions. This includes overseeing the use of performing arts spaces and venues in the COAA , operations and production budgets, and performing arts contracts and purchasing (in consultation with the Dean, Assistant Dean of Business and Personnel, and the Performing Arts Business Services Coordinator). The Associate Dean also works with the Performing Arts Operations Manager to manage the College’s performing arts venues, including Robinson Hall for the Performing Arts, the Johnson Band Center, and the Rowe Arts Building. The Associate Dean for Production Arts + Operations is a member of the College’s leadership team. As such, the Associate Dean collaborates with various COAA leaders, including the Director of Communications & External Relations, the Director of Galleries, the Associate Dean for Academic Affairs, the Associate Dean for Research, the Director of Collaborative Works, and the Director of Assessment. Administrative duties include (but are not limited to) strategic and budget planning and management. The Associate Dean will also maintain a teaching load consisting of a minimum of three (3) credit hours and a maximum of six (6) credit hours per academic year. These courses may include advanced discipline-specific courses (production and/or performing arts), courses in the arts designed for non-majors (which constitute an important aspect of the university’s general education program), and courses offered as part of departmental and/or university honors programs. This position has eleven direct reports, including the Production Manager, two SHRA staff (Performing Arts Operations Manager and Audiences Services Director), two EHRA staff (Costume Lab Manager and Assistant Technical Director), and seven faculty members who complete the CoAA Design & Production Unit. This includes lecturers in Lighting Design, Costume Design, Stage Management, Sound Design, Technical Direction, and a Master Electrician. The Associate Dean for Production Arts + Operations oversees the academic mission of the Design & Production Unit (working in collaboration with the performing arts Chairs and the Production Manager). The Design & Production Unit supports college performances and events both directly and through practicum and introductory courses taught by unit lecturers. Description of the Work The Associate Dean for Production Arts + Operations will Assist the Dean in the oversight of various duties, which may include but aren’t limited to, advancing research missions, supporting the undergraduate and graduate degree programs, faculty and staff evaluation and development, facilitate external support for undergraduate and graduate programs, curriculum development, course schedule planning, performance and event planning, space scheduling and planning, faculty/staff hiring and supervision, budget development, fiscal monitoring, and other related administrative activities with and for the assigned program areas. Reports directly to the Dean and may assume some duties of the Dean in their absence. The Associate Dean also oversees the college’s performing arts spaces and venues with regard to their maintenance, equipment, and renovation. This includes communicating with Legal Affairs, Environmental Health and Safety, Police and Public Safety, and Risk Management and Insurance to ensure that performing arts operations and performances follow university and state policies regarding safety, minors on campus, licenses, contracts, and the use of campus facilities. The Associate Dean will also advise the Dean on other space-related matters in Robinson Hall, including academic, faculty/staff, and support spaces. Required Education, Skills, and Experience UNC Charlotte gives primary consideration to the earned doctorate or terminal degree in one of the performing arts disciplines represented within the College (Dance, Music, or Theatre; this may also include Arts Education). Other disciplines that may be considered include Education or a related degree in the production arts. Demonstrated understanding of the relationship between design and production, performance planning, project management, project budgeting, and/or performing arts operational needs. Minimum Experience/Education UNC Charlotte gives primary consideration to the earned doctorate or terminal degree in the teaching discipline or a related discipline. Preferred Education, Skills and Experience Experience with dance, music, and theatrical productions in an educational setting is preferred. Strong leadership skills and written and oral communication skills are preferred. The ability to manage multiple projects and teams concurrently is also desired. This role will collaboratively manage relationships across multiple disciplines with varying degrees of experience and knowledge; the ability to foster positive, collegial, and professional relationships is highly desired. Necessary Licenses/Certifications Special Notes to Applicants Job Open Date 09/16/2026 Job Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Date Review of Applications will Begin 10/01/2026 Proposed Hire Date 07/01/2027 If time-limited, please indicate appointment end date Contact Information Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this employment opportunity? UNC Charlotte Website HERC Job Board Inside Higher Education Mitratech Circa (formerly known as Local JobNetwork) Another Website Agency Referral Advertisement/Publication Personal Referral Other * Where did you learn about this posting? (Open Ended Question) Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume / Curriculum Vitae Cover Letter / Letter of Interest Contact Information for References Optional Documents Abridged Portfolio Teaching Philosophy Online Portfolio Other Document 9201 University City Blvd \r\nCharlotte, NC 28223 © 2026 The University of North Carolina at Charlotte. All rights reserved. To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://jobs.charlotte.edu/postings/70695": {
      "title": "Position Information",
      "description": "UNC Charlotte Applicant Site | Temporary Clerical Pool-Hourly Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Frequently Asked Questions Application Process State of NC Salary Chart Campus Security Report Student Employment Temporary Clerical Pool-Hourly Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. * This pool is used for the as-needed purpose of hiring an employee for either part-time or full-time temporary employment. Whenever a department has a need for temporary clerical support, this pool will be reviewed to obtain potential candidates. Applicants will be contacted directly by the hiring officials to arrange an interview if qualified. * Applicants in this pool will remain active until 09/30/2026. If you have continued interest in temporary employment beyond that time, you will need to reapply for the next available temporary pool. *Temporary staff employees are ineligible to receive benefits (leave, health coverage, State service credit, etc.). They are, however, covered by Worker's Compensation provisions. However, any non-permanent employee hired to work 30 or more hours per week for three months or more (cumulative of all positions, and not designated as variable hour worker), must be offered the chance to enroll in the High Deductible Health Plan, within 30 days of their eligibility. Any non-permanent employee hired to work less than 30 hours per week (cumulative of all positions, or designated as variable hour worker - i.e. student workers) are measured during designated measurement periods. If they average 30 or more hours per week over a designated measurement period, they will be offered the chance to enroll in the High Deductible Health Plan, and will be notified of instructions. *Special Notes Regarding Eligibility for the High Deductible Health Plan Temporary hourly workers (eligibility based on FTE, duration of more than 30 days) FTE .75+ (cumulative of all jobs) or measured full-time, offered the high deductible health insurance Temporary salaried workers (regardless of the FTE, duration of more than 30 days) Reported as full-time & offered the high deductible health insurance https://hr.charlotte.edu/benefits/benefit-plans/health-insurance The finalist will be subject to a Criminal Background Check and/or a Motor Vehicle Report The Search Committee will not contact references without first verifying permission with the finalist . Please ensure your application reflects the knowledge, skills, abilities, and experiences to support your answers to the supplemental questions. Do not write ‘see resume’ on your application, nor assume the resume can be used as a substitute for when completing the educational history or job duties section. Position Information General Information Position Number STMP15 Working Title Temporary Clerical Pool-Hourly Division Business Affairs Department Human Resources (Adm) Work Unit All departments Work Location Campus Wide Vacancy Open To All Candidates Position Designation Non-Student Temporary Employment Type Temporary - Full-time Hours per week Up to 40 hours per week depending on departmental needs. Work Schedule Normal hours are between 8:00 a.m. – 5:00 p.m., Monday through Friday but may work early morning / weekend / evening hours as may be necessary depending on departmental needs. (Time of shifts may vary) Pay Rate $17.00 an hour or higher. Minimum Experience/Education Completion of high school or equivalency and 1 – 3 years of administrative / clerical office experience. Departmental Preferred Experience, Skills, Training/Education: * Knowledge from the administrative point of view of Banner Student or Banner Financial. * Familiarity with Microsoft Office suite (Word, Excel, Access, Outlook, PowerPoint, etc.). * Familiarity with Google calendar and Google email. Duties and Responsibilities * Responsibilities may include: general office reception, mail distribution, records processing, data entry, filing, correspondence, appointment scheduling, general accounting, etc. * Duties will vary by departmental needs. Other Work/Responsibilities * Excellent oral and written communications skills, combined with a strong customer service orientation are essential. * Attention to detail and the ability to multi-task. Necessary Licenses or Certifications Proposed Hire Date Contact Information Expected Length of Assignment To be determined by departmental needs. Posting Open Date 07/01/2026 Posting Close Date 09/30/2026 Special Notes to Applicants * This pool is used for the as-needed purpose of hiring an employee for either part-time or full-time temporary employment. Whenever a department has a need for temporary clerical support, this pool will be reviewed to obtain potential candidates. Applicants will be contacted directly by the hiring officials to arrange an interview if qualified. * Applicants in this pool will remain active until 09/30/2026. If you have continued interest in temporary employment beyond that time, you will need to reapply for the next available temporary pool. *Temporary staff employees are ineligible to receive benefits (leave, health coverage, State service credit, etc.). They are, however, covered by Worker’s Compensation provisions. However, any non-permanent employee hired to work 30 or more hours per week for three months or more (cumulative of all positions, and not designated as variable hour worker), must be offered the chance to enroll in the High Deductible Health Plan, within 30 days of their eligibility. Any non-permanent employee hired to work less than 30 hours per week (cumulative of all positions, or designated as variable hour worker – i.e. student workers) are measured during designated measurement periods. If they average 30 or more hours per week over a designated measurement period, they will be offered the chance to enroll in the High Deductible Health Plan, and will be notified of instructions. *Special Notes Regarding Eligibility for the High Deductible Health Plan Temporary hourly workers (eligibility based on FTE , duration of more than 30 days) FTE .75+ (cumulative of all jobs) or measured full-time, offered the high deductible health insurance Temporary salaried workers (regardless of the FTE , duration of more than 30 days) Reported as full-time & offered the high deductible health insurance https://hr.charlotte.edu/benefits/benefit-plans/health-insurance The finalist will be subject to a Criminal Background Check and/or a Motor Vehicle Report The Search Committee will not contact references without first verifying permission with the finalist . Please ensure your application reflects the knowledge, skills, abilities, and experiences to support your answers to the supplemental questions. Do not write ‘see resume’ on your application, nor assume the resume can be used as a substitute for when completing the educational history or job duties section. Posting Specific Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you hear about this employment opportunity? UNC Charlotte Website HERC Job Board Inside Higher Education Mitratech Circa (formerly known as Local JobNetwork) Another Website Agency Referral Advertisement/Publication Personal Referral Other * Where did you learn about this posting? (Open Ended Question) * Do you have at least one year of administrative or clerical work experience? No Yes * Have you completed any training or do you have any experience with Microsoft Office Suite i.e. Word, Access, Excel, PowerPoint? No Yes, Beginner Level Yes, Intermediate Level Yes, Advanced Level * Do you have Banner Finance or Banner Student experience? No Yes, Banner Finance experience only. Yes, Banner Student experience only. Yes, Both Banner Finance and Banner Student experience. * Have you completed any training or do you have any professional experience using Google Calendar? No Yes, Beginner Level Yes, Intermediate Level Yes, Advanced Level * Have you completed any training or do you have any professional experience using Google gmail in an office setting (not personal use of gmail)? No Yes, Beginner Level Yes, Intermediate Level Yes, Advanced Level * Do you have at least a high school diploma / GED and one year of related office experience or an equivalent combination of education and experience? Yes - I have at least a high school diploma / GED and one year of related office experience or an equivalent combination of education and experience No - I do not have at least a high school diploma /GED and at least one year of related office experience. Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume / Curriculum Vitae Contact Information for References Optional Documents Cover Letter / Letter of Interest Other Document 9201 University City Blvd \r\nCharlotte, NC 28223 © 2026 The University of North Carolina at Charlotte. All rights reserved. To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
